by nicolas-grekas at 2025-09-04T15:22:14Z

Thanks for chiming in @wouterj

The goal is improved consistency and DX. Maintenance is a second level aspect, let's not focus on this.

When I open the doc and I see multiple tabs everywhere documenting Attribute/PHP/Yaml/XML, that makes me wonder about the complexity we're showing there.

For validation and serialization, we're not ready yet, but once #61545 and #61563 will be made available and adopted, we might deprecate both Yaml and XML. That's a going to be a big win!

For DI and routing, we already have the PHP-DSL. It's proved and stable since many versions now. We use it internally and it provides a nice experience. There's no reasons to keep support for XML at all. That's one less format to tell about, one step in the direction of simplification.

For semantic configuration, we have to keep YAML, because we don't have a better format yet to work with flex.
We added JSON schema for the services, parameters and routes trees, and that improves the DX, but don't be confused: bundles don't need this. Bundles don't do semantic configs. Or if they do, it's via plain PHP arrays in a prepending DI extension, not in XML.

Deprecating XML is mostly for bundle authors. To make the ecosystem settle around one format - the PHP-DSL.
For apps, we can still brainstorm about yaml vs php-dsl vs shapes vs etc. This deprecation doesn't apply to them. (If it does, it's for apps that did decide to use XML for their own config. Bad idea.)

Still strong :+1: here for me.

